# The biggest wake-up call

The current main experiment literally does this:

> task description → two textual tool descriptions → asks the model **“Which tool do you use?”** → parses `A`, `B`, or abstain.

No tool exists. Nothing is invoked. Nothing happens. No task gets completed.

The brand-new cross-family runner still does exactly the same thing: it asks which tool the model would use and calls the answer a tool preference.

That can be a legitimate **L0 psychological probe**:

> Given two textual descriptions, which one does model M report preferring?

It is **not** evidence for:

> Agents prefer evidence-backed tools.

And definitely not:

> Evidence-backed descriptions cause agents to use those tools more.

Those are entirely different estimands.

AgentSearchBench, published in 2026 and directly relevant to your project, reaches essentially the same motivation empirically: textual/semantic relevance and actual execution performance diverge, while execution-aware signals improve agent ranking. ([arXiv][1])

That paper is almost a direct warning against what AgentSEOLab is currently measuring.

