# Your ontology is better than your implementation

This part of `AGENTS.md` is excellent:

```text
SEARCH_RESULT_EXPOSED
SEARCH_RESULT_OPENED
SOURCE_READ
SOURCE_USED
SOURCE_CITED
CAPABILITY_SELECTED
CAPABILITY_INVOKED
EXECUTION_SUCCEEDED
TASK_VERIFIED
```

And it explicitly says these must never be collapsed.

That is exactly the right philosophy.

But the implementation doesn't yet satisfy the philosophy.

For example, your actual field trace classified navigating to a Google search URL as a search-results event, even though the trace says Google was CAPTCHA-blocked.

Similarly, `field.py` mechanically extracts URLs from tool outputs and emits them as `citation` events.

But:

> URL appeared in tool output ≠ source cited
> search engine URL opened ≠ results exposed
> agent mentioned URL ≠ capability selected
> capability selected ≠ invoked
> tool returned HTTP 200 ≠ task succeeded

Your ontology understands this. Your event extractor needs to catch up.

